 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 526006,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "The FDA recommends that you consume less than 0.5 g of caffeine daily. A cup of coffee contains 95 mg of caffeine and a can of soda contains 54 mg. If you drink 2 cups of coffee and 3 cans of soda, have you kept your day’s caffeine consumption to the FDA recommendation? Explain.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>Multiply the caffeine in each drink times the number of drinks you consume. Then add the results.</p>\n<p>Twice the caffeine in coffee + three times the caffeine in soda = your daily intake</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"2(95) + 3(54) = 190 + 162 = 352\\ {\\rm mg = your\\ daily\\ intake}\"></span></p>\n<p><span data-math=\"352\\cancel{{{\\rm{milligrams}}}}\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{1\\ gram}}}}{{1000{\\rm{ }}\\cancel{{{\\rm{milligrams}}}}}}} \\right) = 0.352{\\rm{\\ grams}}\"></span></p>\n<p>FDA recommended maximum = 0.5 grams</p>\n<p>Your daily intake of 0.352 grams is less than the recommended maximum of 0.5 grams.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "Yes, 352 mg of caffeine is equal to 0.352 g, which is less than 0.5 g."
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
